[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Normalization and Cache Eviction in GraphQL Clients"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern GraphQL clients like Apollo Client and Relay use normalized caching to store fetched data efficiently. Understanding how normalization and cache eviction work is critical for building performant applications that avoid stale UI states."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is Normalized Caching?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike document-based caching (which stores the exact JSON response of a query), a normalized cache breaks down incoming query results into individual flat entities. Each entity is assigned a globally unique identifier, typically constructed from its typename and ID (e.g., "User:1" or "Product:42")."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prevents data duplication across different queries."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enables automatic UI updates: modifying an entity in one place updates all active components displaying that entity."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Custom Key Functions"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, clients look for an "id" or "_id" field. If your schema uses composite keys or custom fields, you must configure a custom dataIdFromObject function."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"const client = new ApolloClient({
  link: httpLink,
  cache: new InMemoryCache({
    typePolicies: {
      User: {
        keyFields: ["username"],
      },
    },
  }),
});"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Cache Eviction and Garbage Collection"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Over time, the normalized cache can accumulate unused entities, leading to bloated memory usage. Cache eviction allows you to manually or automatically remove entries from the cache."},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To manually evict an entity in Apollo Client, use the cache.evict method followed by cache.gc() to clean up dangling references."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"client.cache.evict({ id: "Product:123" });
client.cache.gc();"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Cache Maintenance"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always clear or evict specific entities upon mutations like deletions to avoid "ghost" data."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use field policies to define how incoming data merges with existing normalized cache records."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor cache size in production to ensure memory leaks are minimized during heavy user sessions."}]
